module gunbc.rung_drop.determinism_transitive_reachability

import std.types { NonEmptyStr, List }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { GuaranteeRung, MechanicallyPreventable, Mitigatable }
import gunbc.rung_drop { RungDrop, Standing, TypedDeclaration, DeletedWithoutReplacement }

// RUNG DROP — DETERMINISM TRANSITIVE REACHABILITY (2026-09-05).
//
// The fn-arrow corpus_dependency_view composition (mechanism #2 of the §3 consolidation)
// carried fn-arrow declarations across ~25 lens modules whose call graph the
// determinism_transitive_holds witness walked. Deleting corpus_dependency_view removed
// those declarations' subject modules from the fn_arrow_decl_facts_live acquisition
// (they were under src/v2/lens/affected_set, which also contained the deleted modules).
// As a result, determinism_transitive_live_closure_holds can no longer follow callee
// edges into other modules within budget.
//
// The witness has been renamed to determinism_live_intra_holds to reflect that only
// intra-declaration determinism is checked. No cross-module callee traversal occurs.
// This is a real check of each declaration's own structure but does NOT establish
// that the call graph is determinism-safe.
//
// CAUSE CORRECTED BY MEASUREMENT, 2026-09-07. This row previously attributed the budget
// pressure to the NUMBER OF REACHABLE DECLARATIONS, and that attribution is refuted rather
// than refined: the directory-grain walk this drop is gated on reaches FEWER THAN A HUNDRED
// declarations -- barely more than the 78 it seeds from -- and still costs many times the
// per-claim line. The cost is not in the reached set. The wrong sentence was replaced rather
// than annotated, because a restoration trigger is the whole check: a trigger naming a term
// that is not the cost is satisfied by nothing, and it would stand forever with an
// honest-looking receipt attached while anyone chasing it optimised a quantity that does not
// move the number.
//
// AND THE REPLACEMENT SENTENCE WAS ITSELF REFUTED, 2026-09-07, BY THE FIRST LANE THAT TRIED TO
// SATISFY IT. Only the irreducible reasoning is kept here; the executed evidence, the instrument
// that re-derives it, and the prescription all live in `restoration_trigger` below, because a
// second copy of them beside the field would be the fork this row exists to prevent.
//
// WHY A TRIGGER IS WITHDRAWN RATHER THAN WEAKENED. A restoration trigger is a repair
// PRESCRIPTION, not a note. Once the mechanism it was derived from is false, leaving it standing
// as the declared way out with a "that repair did not help" remark beside it is worse than
// deleting it: the next reader follows the prescription, not the remark. So the index prescription
// is removed, and what replaces it is a trigger at COST-SHAPE grain rather than nothing -- the walk
// becoming proportional to what it reaches instead of to the pool it is handed. Naming WHICH
// construct is pool-proportional remains an open question UNDER that trigger and is not a
// precondition for it: a lane that reaches the shape has retired this drop whether or not it can
// say which construct was responsible. The field itself is the authority for both; this annotation
// records only why the withdrawal was a deletion and not a weakening.
//
// WHY A CORRECT FIT CARRYING A FALSE MECHANISM IS THE DANGEROUS ARTIFACT, and the reason this row
// has now been corrected twice. A fit that survives its tests looks like a diagnosis, so the
// mechanism sentence beside it is read as established rather than as the hypothesis it is -- and
// every repair it recruits is aimed at a construct nobody measured. That is not hypothetical here:
// it recruited gunbc#10753, which built the prescribed repair, measured it, and found it worse
// than what it replaced. The fit and the mechanism are therefore carried as SEPARATE claims with
// separate standing, and this row states which is which.
//
// WHY THE NUMBERS ARE NOT WRITTEN DOWN HERE. DESIGN §6 -- name the instrument, never transcribe
// its output. The lane that wrote this correction transcribed a full result table into the field
// below on its first attempt, which would have reproduced, one layer up, exactly the failure this
// row records: a pile of figures unreachable from the thing that owns them, rotting without
// anyone touching either end, while the recipe to re-derive them had been deleted to make room.

// ONE OBSERVATION, AT THE GRAIN IT WAS TAKEN AND NO WIDER. Under claim_batch on a BuildBuddy
// runner the directory-grain walk EXECUTES, COMPLETES AND PASSES -- it is not interrupted there,
// and it costs far more than the per-claim line, which is why the observation is about
// executability rather than about budget. Re-derive the cost by running that claim under
// claim_batch rather than reading a figure here. That establishes executability and says NOTHING
// about the required floor,
// whose disposition for this claim is unpinned and where whether a poll-visible deadline is
// armed the same way is unresolved. AND `interrupted_before_verdict` IS A DISPOSITION SHARED
// ACROSS UNRELATED SUBJECTS, NOT A NAME FOR THIS POPULATION: the same cause appears on main-tip
// floor receipts for outside_modeled_guarantee_witness_test and for
// test.claim.long.carrier_reference_integrity_witness_test, which have nothing to do with this
// walk. A reader must not infer from the cause alone that it describes one subject.
//
// AND THE WITNESS BLOCKS INTERMITTENTLY, WHICH IS THE WORD THAT SURVIVED TWO WRONG ONES. On main
// tip 9b341e1b03 it appears once, in a roster membership list, and NOT on a BLOCKING line; on
// gunbc#10764 -- a pull request of eight lines of Rust comment, touching no .dag at all -- it
// BLOCKS, cause `interrupted_before_verdict`, adjudication REFUSED. Both observations are true, so
// neither "resolved" nor "steadily blocking" is the fact: it is INTERMITTENT, and because #10764
// changes nothing this row reads, the intermittency is a property of MAIN rather than of any
// branch.
//
// THE READING ERROR IS RECORDED BECAUSE IT WAS MADE TWICE IN ONE NIGHT, in both directions, by
// people watching this row: a single green tip was taken as the class being resolved, and a single
// red as it being steady. One tip is one sample of an intermittent process, and consecutive main
// tips are not a controlled comparison -- they differ by whatever else landed. NEITHER OBSERVATION
// IS A COST MEASUREMENT and neither is recorded as one; a witness can stop or start blocking
// because it got cheaper, because the accounting changed, or because something upstream moved.
//
// AN INTERMITTENT BLOCKER IS WORSE THAN A STEADY ONE, WHICH IS WHY THE ATTRIBUTION IS URGENT AND
// NOT A BACKLOG ITEM. A deterministic red gets fixed; a coin-flip red gets rerun, every rerun is a
// full floor job, and the cost is paid by authors of unrelated lanes who have no way to know the
// red is not theirs -- a red on your own PR looks like yours. That is the deferred-cost shape
// DESIGN §1 names: the principal paying is not the one who caused it.
//
// THAT UNCERTAINTY IS RESOLVED AND THIS PARAGRAPH RECORDS ONLY HOW IT WAS RESOLVED. An earlier
// cut said the `interrupted_before_verdict` cause was UNVERIFIED PENDING REQUIRED-FLOOR
// INSTRUMENTATION, on the ground that no run licensing a floor-grain statement had been taken.
// The instrumentation did not have to be built: every completed witnesses run already publishes
// required-floor-claim-cost, so the run had been taken and its receipt was sitting unread. The
// field below is the authority for what that harvest establishes; this annotation does not restate
// it, and the reason the error is kept here rather than deleted is that reasoning about a subject
// an artifact answers directly is the recurring shape this row keeps producing.
data determinism_transitive_reachability: RungDrop = RungDrop {
  identity: "determinism_transitive_reachability" as NonEmptyStr,

  subject: "Transitive determinism reachability over the fn-arrow call graph",

  declared: "2026-09-05",

  standing: Standing,

  declaration: TypedDeclaration {
    previous: MechanicallyPreventable,
    temporary: Mitigatable,
    reason: DeletedWithoutReplacement,
    population: [
      "v2.lens.affected_set.corpus_dependency_view (fn-arrow composition, deleted)",
      "v2.lens.affected_set.entry_selection (fn-arrow declarations, deleted)",
    ],
    restoration_trigger: "THE TRIGGER, STATED ONCE AND AT CAPABILITY GRAIN: the reachability walk's cost becomes proportional to WHAT IT REACHES rather than to the POOL IT IS HANDED, sufficient for determinism_transitive_holds to reach a verdict within the per-claim line at directory grain WITHOUT NARROWING THE SUBJECT. Nothing else retires this row. Everything after this sentence is the evidence for that trigger and the record of what was tried; none of it is a second trigger, and where a later paragraph names an obligation belonging to another subject it says so explicitly and that obligation is carried by its own row. THE TRIGGER WAS REPLACED RATHER THAN WEAKENED, 2026-09-07. It previously prescribed a specific implementation -- that the callee lookup answer BY INDEX rather than by scanning the candidate pool -- and that prescription is refuted by execution below. A trigger naming a REPAIR that does not work is worse than one naming none, because it is satisfiable while the capability stays dead. This one names the COST SHAPE the capability requires, which is what was actually measured, and is satisfiable only by achieving it. The previously declared capability (the callee lookup answering BY INDEX rather than by scanning the candidate pool) is withdrawn because the mechanism it was derived from is refuted by execution. THE REFUTATION, BY REASONING: v2.std.fn_index.decls_in_modules is a plain-valued call with hashable arguments, and the v1 interpreter's eval-frame call memo is on by default (GUNBC_EVAL_MEMO), keyed by fn identity x argument identity -- so the loop-invariant that trigger asked a lane to hoist had already been hoisted by the interpreter, and the per-lookup rescan named as the cost was not being paid. THE REFUTATION, BY MEASUREMENT: gunbc#10753 built exactly the prescribed index and measured it against the unindexed lookup at directory grain under a verdict rule pre-registered before the numbers existed; that PR carries the method, the pre-registration and the figures, and is the citation for them rather than a copy here. Its verdict is that the index is HARMFUL -- it adds cost and removes none -- and that both arms remain far above the per-claim line, so the prescription was not merely insufficient but wrong. WHAT SURVIVES: the O(reached x pool) FIT, unchanged and still good evidence. WHAT DOES NOT: the causal sentence behind it. ATTRIBUTION SO FAR, RE-DERIVED BY NAMING ITS INSTRUMENT RATHER THAN ITS OUTPUT -- run test.claim.long.fn_index_attribution_probe (src/v2/test/claim/long/fn_index_attribution_probe.dag) under claim_batch --source-root dag --source-root src/v2, whose six arms separate acquisition, the closure list, the reachability walk, and the walk plus the determinism fold, and whose last two arms hold the entry and the CLOSURE fixed while enlarging only the candidate POOL so the reached set is unchanged. THE INSTRUMENT IS HOMED WHERE A COMPILE CATCHES ITS ROT, AND THE ARGUMENT THIS ROW CARRIED FOR NOT DOING SO IS WITHDRAWN AS REFUTED BY THE TREE (gunbc#10757, review 62869). It is v2.test.claim.long.fn_index_attribution_probe, stored at src/v2/test/claim/long/fn_index_attribution_probe.dag. This row previously argued that the only discharge for the probe's out-of-closure exposure was adding a root to gunbc.ci_layer_roots.compile_clean_source_roots and to the partition dag_compile_clean_shard_a_witness_test.compile_clean_partition_boundary_matches_compile_clean_source_roots -- a closed roster this lane's brief forbids -- and therefore accepted the exposure as the residue of a discharge it could not perform. THAT WAS WRONG AND THE REPOSITORY ALREADY HELD THE ANSWER: the long home is inside a compiled source root, so a renamed builtin or a changed signature reds like any other module, AND the required floor declines it by authored name through v2.workflow.required_floor long_home_prefixes, so its arms do not run on every push. Both halves, no roster edit. THE EXPOSURE WAS NOT MERELY THEORETICAL WHILE IT STOOD: with the probe under a scratch-probe/ directory the floor refused every arm as declined_changed_witness_outside_discovery the moment a change touched the file, because a planned witness the floor cannot reach is a typed refusal rather than a skip. The wall was right and the reasoning that accepted the exposure was the defect. THE PROBE'S OWN DISSOLUTION, so this instrument does not outlive the question it was built for: it exists ONLY to attribute the cost this row leaves unattributed, and the lane that either retires this row or names the pool-proportional construct DELETES that module in the same change. An instrument that survives its investigation is residue, and this one is not exempt because it is cited here. WHAT DETECTS THE ROT NOW: the compile, on every change whose closure reaches it, which is what the earlier home could not offer. Running the arms is still required before quoting any figure from them, because compiling proves the instrument resolves and never that it still measures what it was built to measure. A LANE THAT FINDS THE INSTRUMENT ROTTED MUST REPAIR IT BEFORE READING THIS ROW'S FINDINGS, because those findings are only as live as the thing that re-derives them. What it establishes: the determinism fold is NOT the cost -- adding determinism_findings over every reached declaration's whole subtree does not rise above run-to-run noise, so a lane optimising it or its concat accumulator will move nothing; and the reachability WALK is pool-sensitive at a fixed reached set, which corroborates the pool term and locates it inside the walk. WHAT REMAINS UNATTRIBUTED, AND IS NOT A SECOND TRIGGER BUT AN OPEN QUESTION UNDER THE ONE ABOVE: WHICH construct inside that walk is pool-proportional, given that the per-lookup rescan is refuted. Naming it would make the repair easier to find; it is not required to retire this row, because the trigger is stated as the shape the cost must reach and a lane that reaches it has retired this drop whether or not it can say which construct was responsible. ONE CANDIDATE WAS PROPOSED HERE AND IS NOW REFUTED BY EXECUTION, RECORDED SO THE NEXT LANE DOES NOT SPEND ITS DISPATCH RE-KILLING IT. The candidate was the eval-frame memo's own keying -- that a probe must derive a key from the pool-sized argument on every call, so removing a pool-sized scan reintroduces a pool-sized cost in the key. Its death condition was written before the arm ran: if the pool sensitivity SURVIVES with GUNBC_EVAL_MEMO=0, keying is not the cost. Run the probe's a/c/e/f arms with the memo off and the sensitivity survives essentially unchanged, so the walk is pool-proportional INTRINSICALLY -- with the memo and without it -- and no repair to keying removes that term. The same arm establishes the opposite of what the candidate assumed: the memo is not a cost here but very nearly the whole performance of this walk, so any later repair in this area must PROTECT it rather than trim it. TWO MECHANISM SENTENCES HAVE NOW DIED ON THIS SUBJECT -- the candidate-pool rescan of gunbc#10744, and this one -- and the fit has survived both, which is why this row states a fit and refuses to state a cause. A third sentence does not go here until an arm supports it. WHAT IS ESTABLISHED IS NOT A MECHANISM BUT A SHAPE, AND THE SHAPE IS THE FINDING: THE WALK'S COST IS PROPORTIONAL TO THE POOL IT IS HANDED RATHER THAN TO WHAT IT REACHES. Three independent measurements, all re-derivable from the named instrument: the reached-set size is pinned EQUAL across a small pool and a pool several times larger, so reach is held constant while cost moves; the walk's cost ratio between those two pools is the same with the eval-frame memo ON as with it OFF. That third one is what makes this a shape rather than an implementation detail -- a cost that tracks the pool identically with a caching tier present and absent is not a property of that tier, of any key, or of any index, but of the WALK, and it is INVARIANT UNDER EVERY MECHANISM PROPOSED AND REFUTED HERE. That invariance is why this row states it as established while stating no cause. A measured cost-shape defect with no carrier is how a defect becomes ownerless, and DESIGN 2's bare-minimum-cost rule prices a PROVEN cost-shape defect as always worth fixing regardless of realized n, without waiting for the cause to be named. Reach-proportional is what this walk should be; pool-proportional is what it measures as. A CONSTRAINT ON ANY FUTURE REPAIR, MEASURED AND RECORDED HERE BECAUSE THE NEXT LANE WILL NOT EXPECT IT: with GUNBC_EVAL_MEMO=0 the same arms run roughly TWO ORDERS OF MAGNITUDE slower. The eval-frame memo is not a cost on this path, it is very nearly the whole performance of it. A later simplification that bypasses or replaces the memo -- which will look clean, because the memo is invisible in the .dag source -- makes this walk about a hundred times slower. Protect it; do not route around it. WHAT THIS ROW DOES NOT CLAIM, having measured it and found otherwise: that the floor claim's own excess over the per-claim line is explained by anything above. Reproduced term by term at the floor's own grain the claim runs at well under half the line, and running it beside deliberately pool-diverse company on one machine does not move it. So the shape defect above is REAL AND IS NOT THE BLOCKER'S EXCESS, and a lane that fixes the shape should expect the intermittency to survive it. AND THE DISPOSITION THAT FOLLOWS IS NOT THE ONE A READER WILL REACH FOR. Learning that the claim's own cost is well under half the line makes a bigger line look reasonable, and it is not: a budget cannot distinguish a claim whose true cost is half the line from one measured AT the line when the environment is separately recorded as swinging by more than a factor of two on BYTE-IDENTICAL evaluator steps. That is a MEASUREMENT TRUSTWORTHINESS defect and not a THRESHOLD defect, and the repair for it is never a bigger number: a number raised to accommodate an unattributed cost stops the refusal firing without stopping the wrongness and destroys the signal that the measurement cannot be trusted, which is the escape hatch DESIGN 5 forbids. A READING, LABELLED AS ONE BECAUSE IT EXPLAINS EVERY ARM AT ONCE AND THAT IS EXACTLY WHEN TO BE CAREFUL: the eval-frame memo is evicted PER WITNESS FRAME, so diversity BETWEEN claims is cleaned up while diversity WITHIN one frame is not. That predicts all three observations without residue -- the floor claim is unmoved when run beside deliberately pool-diverse company, two walks over two pools in ONE frame cost an order of magnitude more than the same two walks in separate frames, and the floor claim is the one-frame one-pool case that never blows up. THE CONSEQUENCE A LATER LANE MUST NOT MISS: that within-frame blowup DOES NOT BEAR ON THIS BLOCKER. Whoever re-derives it will have found something real and will be tempted to call it the cause. Its payer is not retention, refuted by two independent instruments -- an arm holding the walk count fixed while varying the pools retained, and the peak-RSS column, which moves a few percent while the time moves an order of magnitude. Before those two, every arm inferred memory from time, so a timing result and a memory story could not disagree; these can, and do not. A re-deriver must also pin whether each arm re-pays its acquisition or is served from a cross-witness share, which the probe does not settle. AND A RE-DERIVER MUST NOT CARRY ANY FIGURE HERE ONTO THE MACHINE THAT BLOCKS. Every measurement behind this row was taken on remote build executors; the per-claim line is enforced on the required-lane CI runner, which is a different machine population. A distribution measured on the first says nothing about the second, and treating it as though it did is the same error as comparing an isolated figure to a CI figure -- true measurement, wrong subject. THE BLOCKER IS ACCOUNTED FOR, POSITIVELY, ON THE POPULATION WHERE THE LINE IS ENFORCED, AND IT IS NOT THIS SUBJECT. Harvest the retained required-floor claim-cost artifacts from the successful main runs, take this claim's rows, and GROUP THEM BY eval_steps so that work is held constant -- the whole point of the existing pairing authority. AND READ THE ENFORCING POPULATION FROM THE ARTIFACT RATHER THAN FROM A CLOCK: required-floor-claim-cost is published by every completed witnesses run, so this claim's cost on the population that actually enforces it is readable with no push, no lane and no window -- an earlier reading of this row treated a merge freeze as preventing measurement, which confuses producing a new head with reading a receipt from a run that already finished. PARTITION THOSE ROWS BY eval_steps AND BY NOTHING ELSE. eval_steps is deterministic work rather than a clock, so it is immune to runner variance and pool contention, it states the effect of a work-removing repair directly instead of inferring it from a noisy distribution, and it is what makes the partition AUDITABLE: a run classified as pre-repair by its id and date was found to be a post-repair sample only because its eval_steps disagreed with its neighbours. Partitioning by run id, by date or by branch silently mixes the arms and yields a smaller, believable, wrong delta. AND READ THE PRE-REPAIR ARM AS A DISTRIBUTION THAT WORSENS AS IT GROWS, which is the strongest form of the original complaint rather than a restatement of it: as the pre-repair sample grew the top of the range rose and the margin under the 500ms limit fell, with a growing minority of runs landing within 50ms of the line. A shrinking margin under a GROWING sample is not noise -- noise widens a range symmetrically around a stable centre, while this arm's maximum climbs, which is what an intermittent blocker looks like before it becomes a constant one. That is why this class was worth repairing at a realized n that looked survivable. The large majority of recent runs share one step count, and across that group -- at BYTE-IDENTICAL evaluator work, with no change to this claim and none to the tree -- the observed CPU spans a wide factor whose top reaches essentially the whole per-claim ceiling. THE FIGURES ARE NOT WRITTEN HERE ON PURPOSE, and this sentence is the instrument rather than the reading: harvest required-floor-claim-cost from the completed main runs, take this claim's COMPLETED rows, group them by eval_steps, and read the spread and the maximum of the largest group against the limit. A number copied here would be a run-local figure that nothing re-derives, which is the defect this same field names two paragraphs down. A claim whose cost distribution at fixed work already touches its own line does not need a cost defect, a drift, an accounting hole, or any mechanism to refuse intermittently. It needs only to be sampled again. THAT IS THE INTERMITTENCY, and it is why this refuses on changes that touch nothing near it. AND THE ARM IT REFUSES THROUGH IS NOT AN OVERRUN, WHICH MATTERS BECAUSE THE TWO HAVE DIFFERENT DISPOSITIONS AND ONLY ONE OF THEM BLOCKS. Harvest the RED main runs as well as the green ones and this claim's blocking row reads verdict_reached=FALSE with a right-censored cost and a LOWER eval_steps count than any completed run -- fewer steps precisely because it never finished. On red runs where it did finish it came in under budget and those runs were red for other reasons. So the failure mode is INTERRUPTED-BEFORE-VERDICT and not COMPLETED-OVER-BUDGET: the deadline preempts the witness, whether it would have passed is UNKNOWN, and the reported cost is a bound with no upper limit. That is the stronger reason such a row is inadmissible in the distribution above -- not merely that a bound cannot be divided as a cost, but that it is not a sample from that distribution AT ALL, being the cost of a computation that did not happen. AND eval_steps ON A RIGHT-CENSORED ROW IS A TRUNCATION ARTIFACT, NOT A WORKLOAD FIGURE -- one sentence here to save the next harvester an afternoon. An interrupted claim stopped wherever it had got to when the deadline fired, so its step count records how far it got and nothing else: two observed interrupts on this claim truncate at different step counts, both far below every completed run, and neither denotes a workload. The harvest above groups by eval_steps to hold work constant, which is correct ONLY over completed rows. Group the censored ones the same way and they form a third apparent level out of nothing but where the deadline landed -- a bogus population that will look exactly like the drift finding this row already withdrew once. The two arms are the same underlying fact reached differently: a claim whose completed cost distribution touches its ceiling will, on a bad draw, hit the deadline BEFORE finishing, and then it does not report a larger number, it reports no verdict. THE CONSEQUENCE FOR THE REPAIR IS THAT MARGIN IS WORTH MORE THAN IT LOOKS: an overrun is a cost debt on a claim that PASSED, while an interrupt leaves the subject UNDECIDED and is the arm that refuses. Headroom is bought against the second. AND THE REFUSAL BEHAVIOUR OF THAT ARM IS A SEPARATE FILED CLASS, cited here rather than re-derived and deliberately NOT absorbed into this row: gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.non_verdict_disposition_surfaces_as_refusal records that the floor computes the difference between THIS SUBJECT IS WRONG and I DID NOT FINISH LOOKING AT THIS SUBJECT and then discards it at the aggregate boundary, so the red check and the merge gate receive one bit whose only affordance is to run it again. Its specimen is a run refused by interrupted rows in which ZERO claims failed. That is a different defect from this one, it is what makes an interrupt here block an unrelated change, and it is that row's to repair. TWO READINGS WERE PROPOSED AND WITHDRAWN ON THE WAY TO THAT, RECORDED BECAUSE BOTH ARE TEMPTING. A drift -- the claim walking toward the line across a level change -- dissolved once the smaller step-count group was compared against the larger group's actual range rather than against its own mean: the apparent second level sits inside the first population's spread. And a small-sample variance figure understated that spread, which is what made the two groups look separated at all. One error, twice: a sample too small to see its own width. THE REFUSAL IS CORRECT AS A MEASUREMENT AND WRONG AS AN ATTRIBUTION, and the distinction decides the repair. The CPU really was consumed, so the gate is not lying; but that cost is not a property of this claim's code, so charging it to this claim attributes it wrongly. A per-claim threshold enforced on ONE DRAW from a distribution this wide is estimating a statistic it cannot see from a single sample. Raising the ceiling would silence the wrong half and destroy the evidence, which is why no number moves here. THE REPAIR IS MARGIN, AND IT IS THE POOL-PROPORTIONAL SHAPE ALREADY ESTABLISHED ABOVE -- which needs no separate justification, being a DESIGN 2 bare-minimum-cost defect that is fixed regardless of realized n. It was never the blocker and it is still the fix. AND THE ACCEPTANCE TARGET IS DERIVED FROM THE MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN GUESSED: holding the line against a spread of that width requires the MEDIAN to sit below the ceiling divided by the spread, which gives the required reduction directly -- re-derive both the median and the spread from the harvest named above and take the ratio, rather than reading a percentage here. AND THE ACCEPTANCE EVIDENCE IS A DISTRIBUTION, NEVER A GREEN RUN. A claim this near its ceiling under this spread goes green sometimes with nothing repaired, so the first green after any change carries almost no information and is exactly what will be offered as proof. What establishes a repair is a distribution at EQUAL WORK, before and after, on the enforcing population. The before is the harvest described above and it exists today. IF MARGIN CANNOT BE OBTAINED, the remaining honest arm is a further bounded drop naming the shape fix as its trigger at capability grain. Never a bigger ceiling. A RECEIPT FOR THE COST COPRODUCT, RECORDED BECAUSE IT IS A RUNG CLAIM WITH AN EXECUTION BEHIND IT RATHER THAN A DESIGN INTENTION. The observation that opened this investigation was a budget-refused reading -- a lower bound on a cost that never completed, not a cost. It was the most persuasive number available and it was on the correct population, and it is INADMISSIBLE in any ratio. gunbc.floor_cost_distribution refuses it STRUCTURALLY, because its cost reading is a coproduct and a bound has no constructor a cost fits into; no reviewer's judgement had to catch it. That class was filed after a rendered figure just over this same ceiling was read as a completed cost, and it then prevented the identical error in a later lane that knew the story and still reached for the number. AN INSTRUMENT GAP IS DECLARED HERE RATHER THAN PAPERED OVER. The ANALYSIS for the environment-variance question already exists and is not to be re-minted: gunbc.floor_cost_distribution models the cost reading as a coproduct so a censored bound cannot be divided as a cost, and identity_inflation_permille_at_equal_work pairs rows across two runs by identity AND by equal evaluator steps, which is precisely CPU divergence at byte-identical work. What does not exist is an ACTUATOR that runs one claim N times in N processes over one tree and hands the pairs to it, and the actuator that does exist runs only inside CI. So the variance figures recorded elsewhere in the seed were transcribed not because the concept lacked a home but because nothing could RE-RUN it on demand. A NEIGHBOURING CLASS THIS INVESTIGATION SURFACED, FILED IN ITS OWN ROW AND CITED HERE RATHER THAN STORED HERE: gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.inflation_fold_cannot_name_the_environment_it_measures. It is not a trigger of this drop and cannot retire it -- its subject is whether a per-claim cost MEASUREMENT can be trusted, which is a different invalid state with a different harm. It is named here for one reason only: a lane meeting THIS row's trigger must show the cost shape changed, and the evidence for that is a distribution over repeated runs, which is exactly the analysis that class governs. Read it before quoting any such distribution. A repair must be derived from measurement rather than from a plausible mechanism -- that is what the refutations below establish -- but it is the cost SHAPE that must change, not a named construct that must be found first. SUFFICIENT FOR (unchanged, and still the thing being restored): determinism_transitive_holds with pool_roots=['src/v2/lens'] and pool_files=[] reaching a verdict within the per-claim line at directory grain, WITHOUT NARROWING THE SUBJECT -- scoping pool_roots to the modules the walk happens to reach remains the trap and not the repair, because it lowers the number by shrinking the population, leaves the cost shape intact, and re-breaks the moment the subject widens, which is the widening this drop exists to restore. COST SHAPES IN THIS WALK ARE REAL AND ARE EXPLICITLY NOT THE COST, recorded so a later reader does not fix them and believe the defect is gone. THREE OF THEM ARE NOW REPAIRED and are named in the past tense because their symbols no longer resolve: call_reachable_decls_go scanned `reached` linearly per seed through a decl_in_list helper that is deleted, and now answers membership by keyed lookup over a seen map; it appended to `reached` with a copied accumulator and now conses and reverses once at the end; and list_append(rest, callee_decls) copied the worklist every step and is now guarded so an empty callee list does not copy it. REPAIRING THEM DID NOT RETIRE THIS DROP AND WAS NEVER EXPECTED TO: the pool scan in decls_matching_callee is untouched, and this row is retired by the trigger stated at the head of this field -- the cost shape -- and by nothing else. ONE SHAPE IN callees_from_node IS AN ADMITTED, UNRESOLVED OBLIGATION rather than a repair: its duplicate suppression is a linear scan of the accumulated list per callee, which is quadratic in the callees of one declaration and is subject to the same bare-minimum-cost rule. A map-keyed arm was written and measured SLOWER than the list scan at the realized callee counts and was reverted on that measurement; big-O did not grant permission to ship the slower realization, and equally the slower first attempt did not retire the defect it was aimed at. The obligation dissolves when the walk is asked at a grain whose per-declaration callee counts are large enough for the keyed arm to win, and it is recorded here rather than in the source so that it is folded with the rest of this row. THE REMAINING UNREPAIRED SHAPES: determinism_transitive_findings folds with concat(acc, determinism_findings(...)), a copied accumulator that is FREE while the corpus is clean and degrades quadratically exactly when findings appear, so it is invisible in every green run and expensive only when something is already wrong. determinism_live_closure_modules folds list_snoc_item over module_declaration_facts_live(pool_roots: []), a copied accumulator over a list that is empty today. Until the trigger stated at the head of this field is met, determinism over the fn-arrow call graph is unverified. The way out is that trigger and nothing else.",
  }
}
